WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.060
The second pillar, Beneficence, focuses on
minimizing risks and maximizing

2
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.280
scientific validity. Beneficence mandates
that researchers prioritize the

3
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.920
well-being of participants throughout the
study.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:19.460
This requires a precise calculation of the
risk-benefit ratio before any data

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.300
collection begins. A study is only
ethically viable if the potential for

6
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.640
societal and individual good clearly
outweighs the potential for harm.

7
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.320
The final pillar of this framework is
justice.

8
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.860
Justice requires the equitable
distribution of both the burdens and the

9
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.500
benefits of research across the
population.

10
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.440
Fair subject selection is necessary to
ensure that stigmatized or vulnerable

11
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.460
groups are not unfairly targeted for high-
risk studies.

12
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.100
Simultaneously, vulnerable populations
must not be denied access to the

13
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.620
benefits of the eventual research
findings.

14
00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:00.400
Institutional responsibility demands total
transparency and accountability to

15
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.600
these three core principles. Independent
research ethics committees provide

16
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.740
the rigorous oversight needed to ensure
these standards are met globally.

17
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.900
This oversight is most effective when
paired with active community engagement

18
00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.320
to protect targeted demographics.

19
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.740
Ethical research relies on an active,
continuous defense of human dignity

20
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.520
sustained by rigorous frameworks and
independent oversight.
